    "durationMs": 173326,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener is complete and fully verified. Here's what I built:\n\n**Implementation** (Go 1.24.3, chi v5.2.1, pgx v5.7.6):\n- `main.go` — reads `DATABASE_URL` from the env, connects to Postgres, applies migrations, serves on `0.0.0.0:8000`\n- `migrations/001_init.sql` + `migrate.go` — embedded, idempotent migrations applied at startup before serving\n- `store.go` — pgx-backed persistence; redirect clicks increment atomically via a single `UPDATE ... RETURNING`\n- `handlers.go` — chi routes for all four endpoints with the required status codes\n- `start.sh` — builds and runs in the foreground (deps were installed during development, not in the script); README documents startup\n\n**Verified end-to-end against the spec:**\n- POST auto/custom codes → 201 with `{\"code\",\"url\",\"clicks\":0}`, submitted URL returned unchanged\n- Duplicate custom code → 409; malformed JSON, missing/invalid url (`https://`, `ftp://`), bad codes (`abc`, `bad code!`) → 422\n- GET by code → 200/404; list → creation-ordered `{\"items\", \"total\"}`\n- `/r/{code}` → 302 with correct `Location`, clicks incremented exactly once (0→1→4 across separate checks), unknown codes → 404\n- Restarted the server: links and click counts survived\n\nThe database is left clean and no server processes are running, so `sh start.sh` gives the test suite a fresh start."
  }
